WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.560
GI Bill mortgages raised home ownership from 44% to 62%, steering veterans into car-only suburbs. This is unintended consequences, episode 168, a wartime promise of a house, the tracks that made the payment work, the driving that followed, and a layout later laws could not move. After the Great Depression left more than half of United States mortgages in default, and wartime production had drawn millions into uniform, Congress looked for a way back to civilian life

00:00:29.560 --> 00:00:32.940
that would not replay the foreclosure wave of 1932.

00:00:33.320 --> 00:00:39.160
On June 22, 1944, lawmakers passed the Service Men's Readjustment Act.

00:00:39.660 --> 00:00:43.700
They meant to reward 16 million returning servicemen with practical tools,

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:45.520
not a parade and a handshake.

00:00:46.160 --> 00:00:48.840
The home loan piece ran through the Veterans Administration.

00:00:49.320 --> 00:00:54.040
The Veterans Administration promised private lenders repayment if a veteran defaulted.

00:00:54.040 --> 00:00:59.300
With that promise, banks could offer a 30-year amortized mortgage and no-down payment.

00:00:59.840 --> 00:01:03.340
Nobody in that design was asking the federal government to build the houses.

00:01:03.980 --> 00:01:09.120
Supporters saw the loans as the next step in credit support already begun in the 1930s

00:01:09.120 --> 00:01:13.020
by the Home Owner's Loan Corporation and the Federal Housing Administration.

00:01:13.620 --> 00:01:15.160
The arithmetic felt plain.

00:01:15.880 --> 00:01:20.920
A zero-down, 30-year term cut the monthly payment enough that a factory wage could cover

00:01:20.920 --> 00:01:26.200
principle and interest on a modest new house. Five-year balloon mortgages had never allowed

00:01:26.200 --> 00:01:31.720
that. Lenders put the new guarantees where large-scale building was easiest. Farmland

00:01:31.720 --> 00:01:37.960
outside city limits. On Long Island, developer William Levitt began Levittown in 1947.

00:01:38.660 --> 00:01:43.560
Assembly line methods turned out thousands of Cape Cod houses at prices low enough for veterans

00:01:43.560 --> 00:01:49.800
to buy with GI Bill financing. Similar tracks rose in New Jersey and Pennsylvania. Early

00:01:49.800 --> 00:01:55.360
Reports from the Veterans Administration showed rapid uptake. By the early 1950s, millions

00:01:55.360 --> 00:02:00.640
of loans had closed. Proponents pointed to new neighborhoods as proof the program worked.

00:02:01.100 --> 00:02:05.700
Few planners asked whether those locations would state tied to jobs once streetcar and

00:02:05.700 --> 00:02:10.500
commuter rail service thinned. Builders could buy acreage at agricultural prices,

00:02:11.020 --> 00:02:16.260
grade at once, and poor concrete slabs in sequence. Each step lowered the per-unit cost.

00:02:16.260 --> 00:02:21.020
The Veterans Administration guarantee then turned that lower cost into a monthly

00:02:21.020 --> 00:02:26.100
payment a buyer could meet. The loans financed new construction rather than existing urban

00:02:26.100 --> 00:02:31.340
stock, so capital went to Greenfield sites where land was cheap and rail lines were absent

00:02:31.340 --> 00:02:36.700
or distant. Levitt Town and its imitators grew as clusters of single-family houses served

00:02:36.700 --> 00:02:43.500
only by roads. The 1956 Federal Highway Act then added 41,000 miles of interstate routes.

00:02:43.500 --> 00:02:48.760
Those roads made the new tracks reachable by car and further rewarded the outward pattern.

00:02:49.160 --> 00:02:52.620
As white veterans moved, northern cities lost tax base.

00:02:53.160 --> 00:02:56.500
Suburban municipalities put money into roads instead of transit.

00:02:57.120 --> 00:03:00.460
Rail operators facing declining ridership cut service.

00:03:01.060 --> 00:03:04.140
The next wave of buyers found a car even harder to do without.

00:03:04.680 --> 00:03:09.800
Families without access to the GI bill benefits, including most black veterans denied loans

00:03:09.800 --> 00:03:14.820
by discriminatory bank practices, remained in cities whose infrastructure received less

00:03:14.820 --> 00:03:20.640
new investment. Jobs, schools, and stores ended up spaced for automobile travel rather than

00:03:20.640 --> 00:03:26.400
walking or rail. Estimates from an American Economic Association study attribute roughly

00:03:26.400 --> 00:03:31.580
one quarter of the homeownership rise among affected cohorts directly to the timing and terms of

00:03:31.580 --> 00:03:37.880
these loans. A fair objection is that families simply preferred detached houses. Pre-war streetcar

00:03:37.880 --> 00:03:42.340
suburbs had already offered that option at higher densities and with rail access.

00:03:43.020 --> 00:03:47.660
What the GI Bill Terms removed was the price penalty that had once kept development closer

00:03:47.660 --> 00:03:48.740
to existing lines.

00:03:49.460 --> 00:03:54.040
The guarantee lowered the buyer's monthly cost and also took away any reason for a builder

00:03:54.040 --> 00:03:56.300
to pay a premium for land near a station.

00:03:56.960 --> 00:04:01.800
The Veterans Administration did not require proximity to transit as a condition of the guarantee.

00:04:01.800 --> 00:04:07.920
By the late 1960s, the social costs of that car-dependent sprawl were visible enough that

00:04:07.920 --> 00:04:12.680
federal policy began to shift, congestion, air quality, and uneven access.

00:04:13.420 --> 00:04:19.400
The 1968 Fair Housing Act and later transit funding measures tried to open suburban opportunities

00:04:19.400 --> 00:04:21.380
and to support alternatives to driving.

00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:25.020
The physical layout of the tracks already built stayed fixed.

00:04:25.580 --> 00:04:30.860
Later mortgage programs kept favoring single-family detached homes, so the same incentive structure

00:04:30.860 --> 00:04:37.760
remained. Today the majority of United States housing units built between 1945 and 1970 still

00:04:37.760 --> 00:04:43.320
sit in low-density corridors whose residents drive for most trips. Putting rail or bus rapid

00:04:43.320 --> 00:04:48.540
transit into those corridors now meets a higher per-rider cost because the houses are spaced

00:04:48.540 --> 00:04:53.920
far apart. That cost is the arithmetic of the site selection logic the loans first rewarded.

00:04:54.875 --> 00:04:59.595
A housing credit rule that never asks where the house sits will keep favoring whichever site is

00:04:59.595 --> 00:05:04.675
cheapest to build on this year. Watch the same pull wherever mortgage terms or tax treatment

00:05:04.675 --> 00:05:09.115
pay more for a new detached unit than for infill beside a station that already runs.

00:05:09.895 --> 00:05:14.375
The lock-in does not need a century. It can finish inside one generation of buyers.

00:05:15.035 --> 00:05:19.675
The sister show modern investing techniques is a useful place to sit with how a credit rule,

00:05:19.975 --> 00:05:25.635
not a slogan decides which land gets the money. How might today's housing programs change if every

00:05:25.635 --> 00:05:29.295
loan guarantee carried a simple test for proximity to frequent transit.

00:05:29.895 --> 00:05:31.675
And that's where today's story leaves us.

00:05:32.095 --> 00:05:35.535
If it changed how you see one everyday decision, that's the whole point.

